format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

objective u1-stable-unstable
statement: Know that some combinations of protons and neutrons make a stable nucleus and others make an unstable one.
misconception: Every atom stays the same for ever.

item u1-l5-predict-stable
type: predict
surfaces: lesson

prompt:
    Does every atom stay exactly the same for ever?

input: choice

options:
    {"id":"yes","label":"Yes, every atom stays the same"}
    {"id":"no","label":"No, some atoms can change"}

answer: no

explanation:
    Your guess is not scored. Find out in the next cards.

item u1-l5-sort-stable
type: sort
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

prompt:
    Which of these atoms are stable? Sort each one.

buckets:
    {"id":"stable","label":"Stable","icon":"AqStable"}
    {"id":"unstable","label":"Unstable","icon":"AqUnstable"}

items:
    {"id":"c12","label":"Carbon-12","bucket":"stable"}
    {"id":"c14","label":"Carbon-14","bucket":"unstable"}
    {"id":"o16","label":"Oxygen-16","bucket":"stable"}
    {"id":"u238","label":"Uranium-238","bucket":"unstable"}

explanation:
    Carbon-12 and oxygen-16 are stable. Carbon-14 and uranium-238 are unstable, which means radioactive.

item u1-l5-tom-all-stable
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

statement:
    Every atom stays the same for ever.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Most atoms around us do stay the same, but an unstable nucleus can change.

item u1-l5-tom-body-stable
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-potassium-40

statement:
    Most of the atoms in your body are stable.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Almost all of your atoms are stable. A tiny number are naturally unstable, such as potassium-40.

item u1-x-stable-meaning
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

prompt:
    What does it mean for a nucleus to be stable?

options:
    {"id":"stays","label":"It stays the same"}
    {"id":"changes","label":"It is likely to change","note":"That describes an unstable nucleus."}
    {"id":"gone","label":"It has no protons","note":"Every nucleus has protons."}

answer: stays

explanation:
    A stable nucleus stays the same. An unstable nucleus can change.

item u1-x-unstable-which
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

prompt:
    Which of these atoms is unstable?

options:
    {"id":"c14","label":"Carbon-14"}
    {"id":"c12","label":"Carbon-12","note":"Carbon-12 is stable."}
    {"id":"o16","label":"Oxygen-16","note":"Oxygen-16 is stable."}

answer: c14

explanation:
    Carbon-14 is unstable, which means radioactive.
